/// mlrepro — `flatten` (a recursive-generic transformer that recurses over `GIter(GIter a)`, chaining the
/// inner iterators) composes at ONE element type but DECLINES at TWO. The residual after the self-nested-
/// producer re-entrancy fix (`5b7b4d575`). (2026-07-16, v-iterators; v-inference's stated next target)
///
/// PROGRESS: the self-nested-producer re-entrancy fix (5b7b4d575 — memo guard skips a re-entrant nested-Any
/// data element) UNBLOCKED the producer/consumer nested case: `count(from-list([from-list([1,2]),
/// from-list([3,4,5])]))` → 2, and `flatten` at a SINGLE element type now compiles + RUNS (icount → 5).
///
/// RESIDUAL (this repro): `flatten` at TWO element types in one program still declines CDZ0201. `flatten`
/// recurses over `GIter(GIter a)` and `chain`s each inner onto the flattened rest — a recursive-generic
/// TRANSFORMER whose element is itself a `GIter a` (nested generic). Instantiated at `a = Int64` AND
/// `a = String` in one program, the nested element type isn't tied across the two instantiations. This is
/// the recursive-transformer-over-a-nested-generic-element tie — sibling of the flat recursive-transformer
/// closure tie, one level of nesting deeper.
///
/// ISOLATION:
///   - flatten at ONE element type (Int only) → COMPILES + RUNS (icount → 5). Self-nested producer fix holds.
///   - flatten at TWO element types (Int + String) in one program → CDZ0201 (this file).
///   - the direct producer/consumer nested case (no flatten recursion) → FINE at ≥1 type (5b7b4d575).
///   - CHECK vs LOWER: `cdz check` PASSES (exit 0); declines at LOWERING (the monomorphizer can't specialize
///     the nested element at the second instantiation).
///
/// OWNER: v-inference (they asked "re-run the 22 recursive-generic todos + tell me which remain"; this is the
/// one that remains — the nested-element transformer tie, their next target). EXPECTED when fixed: flatten
/// composes at ≥2 element types like the other giter.cdz transformers, and lands in giter.cdz as `flatten`.

type GIter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, GIter(a))

def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)

def chain(a, b) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => b
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(h, chain(rest, b))

/// Recurses over GIter(GIter a); nested-generic element.
def flatten(its) = match its with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(inner, rest) => chain(inner, flatten(rest))

/// FACE 1: Int element. flatten ALONE at Int compiles + runs (icount → 5).
@test
def flatten-ints() =
  if count(flatten(from-list([from-list([1, 2]), from-list([3, 4, 5])]))) == 5 then unit else trap("flatten ints")

/// FACE 2: String element, SAME PROGRAM. The second nested-element instantiation triggers CDZ0201.
@test
def flatten-strings() =
  if count(flatten(from-list([from-list(["a"]), from-list(["b", "c"])]))) == 3 then unit else trap("flatten strings")
